  "live_records": {
    "q-32107": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32107",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If $5{b}^{2}-15b+10=5(b+p)(b+q),$ then $p+q=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{2}-15b+10 & =5({b}^{2}-3b+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${b}^{2}-3b+2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(2).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-2) & =-3 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3b=-b-2b$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-3b+2 & = \\\\ {b}^{2}-b-2b+2 & = \\\\ ({b}^{2}-b)+(-2b+2) & = \\\\ b(b-1)-2(b-1) & = \\\\ (b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({b}^{2}-3b+2) & =5(b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-2,q=-1,$ and $p+q=-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
