    "q-2855": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2855",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$5{t}^{2}-15t-20=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2}-15t-20 & =5({t}^{2}-3t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${t}^{2}-3t-4$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-4).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-4) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 4 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-4) & =-3 & ✓ \\\\ (-1)+4 & =3 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-2) & =0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3t=t-4t$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t-4 & = \\\\ {t}^{2}+t-4t-4 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+t)+(-4t-4) & = \\\\ t(t+1)-4(t+1) & = \\\\ (t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =5(t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
