    "q-32105": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32105",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-32105-a",
