    "q-80173": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80173",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Factor $6-3x-3{x}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that we can factor out a $(-3)$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3x-3{x}^{2} & =-3({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${x}^{2}+x-2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(-2).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-2) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-2) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ (-1)+(2) & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $x=-x+2x$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ -3({x}^{2}-x+2x-2) & = \\\\ -3(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ -3(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ -3(x+2)(x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
